const EXPLAIN={
1:{"how": ["Multiply the numbers: 2 × 5 = <b>10</b>.", "Same base, multiplying: <b>add</b> the exponents: 4 + 8 = <b>12</b>.", "10<var>m</var><sup>12</sup>. That is B."], "why": {"0": "The 2 and 5 multiply (2 × 5 = 10). They don’t add.", "2": "2 × 5 = 10, and the exponents add (4 + 8 = 12), they don’t multiply.", "3": "When you multiply powers with the same base, the exponents add: 4 + 8 = 12, not 4 × 8 = 32."}, "right": "Multiply the numbers, add the exponents.", "numworks": "<b>Calculation</b>: try m = 2. Type 2×2^(4)×5×2^(8) and 10×2^(12) (after each exponent, press the right arrow key to come back down). Both show 40960."},
2:{"how": ["<var>g</var>(<var>x</var>) = <var>f</var>(<var>x</var>) − 23 takes every <var>y</var>-value of <var>f</var> and subtracts 23.", "Subtracting outside the function moves the graph <b>down</b> 23 units.", "The slope doesn’t change (still 1), so the two lines are <b>parallel</b>. That is D."], "why": {"0": "The slope is the same, so the lines are parallel. And −23 moves the graph down, not up.", "1": "The direction is right (down), but the slope didn’t change, so the lines are parallel.", "2": "Parallel is right, but −23 moves the graph down, not up."}, "right": "+ or − on the outside moves up or down; same slope means parallel.", "desmos": "Type <b>y = x</b> and <b>y = x − 23</b>. The second line is the same tilt, 23 lower."},
3:{"how": ["The <b><var>x</var>-intercepts</b> are where the graph crosses the <b><var>x</var>-axis</b> (<var>y</var> = 0).", "The curve crosses the <var>x</var>-axis at <b>(−1, 0)</b> and <b>(3, 0)</b>.", "Select those two circles."], "miss": "Only the points on the <var>x</var>-axis count. (1, 4) is the vertex (the top), and (0, 3) is the <var>y</var>-intercept. You earn 1 point for each correct point, and an extra pick takes one away.", "right": "<var>x</var>-intercepts sit on the <var>x</var>-axis, where <var>y</var> = 0."},
4:{"how": ["10 brushes total: <b><var>x</var> + <var>y</var> = 10</b>.", "“Small was 4 times large”: small = 4 × large, so <b><var>x</var> = 4<var>y</var></b>.", "That is A."], "why": {"1": "The brushes add up to 10 (a total), so it is <var>x</var> + <var>y</var> = 10, not a difference.", "2": "The total is right, but small is 4 times large: <var>x</var> = 4<var>y</var>. <var>y</var> = 4<var>x</var> says large is 4 times small.", "3": "Both are backwards: the total adds (<var>x</var> + <var>y</var> = 10), and small = 4 × large is <var>x</var> = 4<var>y</var>."}, "right": "“Total” means add. “A is 4 times B” means A = 4B.", "check": "Try 8 small and 2 large: 8 + 2 = 10 and 8 = 4(2). ✓"},
5:{"how": ["The <var>y</var>-intercept is the number in front: <b>3</b>. The graph crosses the <var>y</var>-axis at (0, 3).", "The base <span class=\"frac\"><span>2</span><span>3</span></span> is less than 1, so the graph goes <b>down</b> as <var>x</var> grows (decay).", "Starts at 3 and decreases: that is C."], "why": {"0": "This one decreases, but it crosses the y-axis at 1, not 3. The 3 in front is the y-intercept.", "1": "This one crosses at 1 and goes up. A base less than 1 means the graph goes down.", "3": "It crosses at 3, but a base of 2/3 (less than 1) means decay: the graph should go down, not up."}, "right": "a is where it crosses the y-axis; b less than 1 means it goes down.", "desmos": "Type <b>y = 3(2/3)^x</b>. It crosses the y-axis at 3 and goes down to the right.", "numworks": "<b>Grapher</b>: Add an element, type 3(2/3)^x (press the right arrow after the exponent), open the Graph tab. It crosses at (0, 3) and falls to the right."},
6:{"how": ["Turn each solution into a factor: <var>x</var> = <span class=\"frac\"><span>7</span><span>5</span></span> means 5<var>x</var> − 7 = 0, and <var>x</var> = −<span class=\"frac\"><span>2</span><span>3</span></span> means 3<var>x</var> + 2 = 0.", "Multiply: (5<var>x</var> − 7)(3<var>x</var> + 2) = 15<var>x</var><sup>2</sup> + 10<var>x</var> − 21<var>x</var> − 14 = <b>15<var>x</var><sup>2</sup> − 11<var>x</var> − 14</b>.", "That is B."], "why": {"0": "The factors are (5x − 7)(3x + 2), which starts with 15x², not 14x².", "2": "The factors are (5x − 7)(3x + 2): 5 × 3 = 15 in front and −7 × 2 = −14 at the end.", "3": "The front and end are right, but the middle is 10x − 21x = −11x, not +11x."}, "right": "x = p/q gives the factor qx − p. Then multiply the factors.", "numworks": "<b>Equations</b>: type the equation (with =), then <b>Solve the equation</b>: 15x² − 11x − 14 = 0 gives x = 7/5 and x = −2/3."},
7:{"how": ["In <var>f</var>(<var>x</var>) = 320(0.90)<sup><var>x</var></sup>, the number in front, <b>320</b>, is the starting value (week 0): the initial price was <b>$320</b>.", "The base 0.90 is less than 1, so the price <b>decreases</b>.", "0.90 means the price keeps 90% each week, so it goes down by 100% − 90% = <b>10%</b> each week."], "miss": "The first number (320) is the starting price. A base under 1 means decrease. The percent change is 1 − 0.90 = 0.10, so 10%, not 90% (90% is what is kept each week).", "right": "a = start. b under 1 = decrease. Rate = 1 − b.", "check": "Week 1: 320 × 0.90 = 288, which is 32 less than 320, and 32 ÷ 320 = 10%. ✓"},
8:{"how": ["Find the biggest perfect square inside 75: 75 = <b>25</b> × 3.", "√75 = √25 · √3 = <b>5√3</b>. That is A."], "why": {"1": "15√5 = √(225 · 5) = √1125, way too big. Look for a perfect square that divides 75: 25.", "2": "3√5 = √45, not √75. Split 75 as 25 × 3, and the 25 comes out as 5.", "3": "5√15 = √375. 75 = 25 × 3, so the 25 becomes 5 outside and 3 stays inside."}, "right": "Pull out the biggest perfect square.", "numworks": "<b>Calculation</b>: type √(75). It shows 5√3 (and 8.66 as a decimal)."},
9:{"how": ["Pick two points on the line: (0, 60) and (10, 0).", "<b>Run</b>: 10 − 0 = 10 minutes. <b>Rise</b>: 0 − 60 = −60 liters.", "Rate = −60 ÷ 10 = <b>−6 L/min</b>. Negative because the tank is draining. That is A."], "why": {"1": "The line goes down (the tank is draining), so the rate is negative.", "2": "This is upside down: liters go on top, minutes on the bottom. 60 ÷ 10 = 6, not 10 ÷ 60.", "3": "Upside down and the wrong sign. Rise over run: −60 ÷ 10 = −6."}, "right": "Rise over run; going down means negative.", "desmos": "Add a table with (0, 60) and (10, 0), then use <b>Add Regression</b> (linear): m = −6.", "numworks": "<b>Regression</b>: put x in X1 and y in Y1, open the Graph tab, press OK and pick <b>Linear ax+b</b>. The Stats tab shows a = −6."},
10:{"how": ["Distribute: 12 − 4<var>w</var> &gt; 2<var>w</var> − 36.", "<b>Slide</b> the <var>w</var>-terms together: add 4<var>w</var> to both sides: 12 &gt; 6<var>w</var> − 36. Then add 36: 48 &gt; 6<var>w</var>.", "<b>Slice</b> by 6: 8 &gt; <var>w</var>, which is <b><var>w</var> &lt; 8</b>. That is C."], "why": {"0": "The number 8 is right, but the sign got flipped. 48 > 6w means w is less than 8. (You only flip when you multiply or divide by a negative.)", "1": "2(w − 18) is 2w − 36, not 2w − 18. Distribute the 2 to both terms.", "3": "Distribute the 2 to the 18 too (2w − 36), and check the direction of the sign."}, "right": "Distribute first, slide, then slice. Flip only when dividing by a negative.", "check": "Try w = 0: 12 > −36 is true, so the answer must include 0. w < 8 does. ✓", "desmos": "Type <b>y = 12 − 4x</b> and <b>y = 2(x − 18)</b>. Click where they cross: (8, −20). The first line is higher to the left of x = 8, so x < 8."},
11:{"how": ["Look for two binomials that multiply to 2<var>x</var><sup>2</sup> + <var>x</var> − 15: the fronts multiply to 2<var>x</var><sup>2</sup> and the ends multiply to −15.", "Try (2<var>x</var> − 5)(<var>x</var> + 3): 2<var>x</var><sup>2</sup> + 6<var>x</var> − 5<var>x</var> − 15 = 2<var>x</var><sup>2</sup> + <var>x</var> − 15. ✓", "The factors are <b>{x} + 3</b> and <b>2{x} − 5</b>: A and D."], "why": {"1": "(2x − 3)(x + 5) = 2x² + 7x − 15. The middle should be just x.", "2": "(x + 5)(2x − 3) = 2x² + 7x − 15. The middle should be just x.", "4": "(x − 3)(2x + 5) = 2x² − x − 15. The middle sign is wrong.", "5": "(2x + 5)(x − 3) = 2x² − x − 15. The middle sign is wrong."}, "right": "Multiply your two factors back out to check the middle term.", "numworks": "<b>Equations</b>: type the equation (with =), then <b>Solve the equation</b>: 2x² + x − 15 = 0 gives x = −3 and x = 5/2, which match the factors (x + 3) and (2x − 5)."},
12:{"how": ["<b>Domain</b> is the <var>x</var>-values. Read the graph from <b>left to right</b>.", "The graph starts at the closed dot (−1, 2), so <var>x</var> = −1 is included, and the arrow means it keeps going right forever.", "All real numbers greater than or equal to −1. That is A."], "why": {"1": "1 is the lowest y-value (the bottom of the curve), so that is about range. Domain uses the x-values, which start at −1.", "2": "The graph doesn’t go forever to the left. It starts at the dot at x = −1.", "3": "The arrow means the graph keeps going to the right, so there is no right-hand stopping point at 5."}, "right": "Domain: left to right. A dot is a stop, an arrow keeps going."},
13:{"how": ["The line crosses the <var>y</var>-axis at <b>(0, 2)</b>, so <var>b</var> = 2.", "From (0, 2) go <b>right 2</b>, <b>up 1</b> to (2, 3): slope = <span class=\"frac\"><span>1</span><span>2</span></span>.", "<var>y</var> = <span class=\"frac\"><span>1</span><span>2</span></span><var>x</var> + 2. That is D."], "why": {"0": "The line crosses the y-axis at 2, not −4 (that is the x-intercept). And it rises 1 for every 2 to the right, so the slope is 1/2.", "1": "The y-intercept is right, but the slope is 1/2 (up 1 for every 2 right), not 2.", "2": "The slope is right, but the line crosses the y-axis at 2. −4 is where it crosses the x-axis."}, "right": "b is where it crosses the y-axis; slope is rise over run.", "desmos": "Type <b>y = (1/2)x + 2</b>. It crosses the y-axis at 2 and the x-axis at −4, like the picture."},
14:{"how": ["Factor out 3 first: 12<var>x</var><sup>2</sup> − 36<var>x</var> + 27 = 3(4<var>x</var><sup>2</sup> − 12<var>x</var> + 9).", "4<var>x</var><sup>2</sup> − 12<var>x</var> + 9 is a perfect square: (2<var>x</var> − 3)<sup>2</sup>. So <var>f</var>(<var>x</var>) = 3(2<var>x</var> − 3)<sup>2</sup>.", "One zero: 2<var>x</var> − 3 = 0 gives <var>x</var> = <span class=\"frac\"><span>3</span><span>2</span></span>. That is D."], "why": {"0": "3(2x − 3)(2x + 3) = 12x² − 27, with no middle term. The function has −36x, so it is a perfect square, not a difference of squares.", "1": "3(2x − 3)(2x + 3) = 12x² − 27, not 12x² − 36x + 27.", "2": "The factoring is right, but 2x − 3 = 0 gives x = 3/2, not 2/3."}, "right": "Factor out the number first; a² − 2ab + b² is (a − b)².", "desmos": "Type <b>y = 12x^2 − 36x + 27</b>. It touches the x-axis at one point, (1.5, 0), and 1.5 = 3/2.", "numworks": "<b>Equations</b>: type the equation (with =), then <b>Solve the equation</b>: 12x² − 36x + 27 = 0 gives x = 3/2 only."},
15:{"how": ["The line crosses the <var>y</var>-axis at <b>(0, −4)</b>: the <var>y</var>-intercept is <b>−4</b>.", "From (0, −4) go <b>right 1</b>, <b>up 2</b> to (1, −2): slope = <b>2</b>.", "Slope: 2. {y}-intercept: −4."], "miss": "The y-intercept is where the line crosses the y-axis (−4), not the x-axis (2). Slope is rise over run: up 2 for every 1 right, so 2, not 0.5.", "right": "y-intercept: where it crosses the y-axis. Slope: rise over run.", "desmos": "Type <b>y = 2x − 4</b>. It matches the picture: through (0, −4) and (2, 0)."},
16:{"how": ["Distribute each term of (<var>z</var> + 5) to all three terms: <var>z</var>(<var>z</var><sup>2</sup> − 4<var>z</var> + 6) + 5(<var>z</var><sup>2</sup> − 4<var>z</var> + 6).", "<var>z</var><sup>3</sup> − 4<var>z</var><sup>2</sup> + 6<var>z</var> + 5<var>z</var><sup>2</sup> − 20<var>z</var> + 30.", "Combine: <var>z</var><sup>3</sup> + <b><var>z</var><sup>2</sup></b> − <b>14<var>z</var></b> + 30. That is C."], "why": {"0": "The z² terms are −4z² + 5z² = z², and the z terms are 6z − 20z = −14z. Every term of z + 5 has to multiply every term of the trinomial.", "1": "The product of a binomial and a trinomial starts with z³ (z times z²). This is too small.", "3": "The product starts with z³. This looks like an attempt to add or subtract the two expressions instead of multiplying."}, "right": "Each term of the first times each term of the second, then combine.", "check": "Try z = 1: (1 + 5)(1 − 4 + 6) = 6 · 3 = 18, and 1 + 1 − 14 + 30 = 18. ✓", "numworks": "<b>Calculation</b>: try z = 1. Type (1 + 5)(1^(2) − 4×1 + 6) and 1^(3) + 1^(2) − 14×1 + 30 (after each exponent, press the right arrow key). Both show 18."},
17:{"how": ["The highest point (vertex) is <b>(−3, 9)</b>.", "The <b>axis of symmetry</b> is the vertical line through the vertex: <b><var>x</var> = −3</b> (VUX). That is D.", "Check the others: it crosses the <var>x</var>-axis at −6 and 0, and 9 is the <b>maximum</b>, not the minimum."], "why": {"0": "The zeros are where it crosses the x-axis: −6 and 0. There is nothing at x = 6.", "1": "9 is the highest point, so it is the maximum value, not the minimum. The graph opens down.", "2": "The coordinates are switched. The vertex is (−3, 9)."}, "right": "Axis of symmetry: x = the x-value of the vertex (VUX).", "desmos": "Type <b>y = −(x + 3)^2 + 9</b>. Click the graph: the top is (−3, 9), and the x-intercepts are (−6, 0) and (0, 0), like the picture."},
18:{"how": ["The <var>y</var>-intercept of an exponential model is the value at <var>x</var> = 0, which is one step <b>before</b> year 1.", "Each year the count grows by about 1.3 to 1.4 times (27 → 35 → 45 …). Working back one step from 27 gives about 19, not 27.", "So the model with start 18.9 and growth 1.4 fits: <var>f</var>(<var>x</var>) = 18.9(1.4)<sup><var>x</var></sup>. That is C.", "Check: 18.9(1.4)<sup>1</sup> ≈ 26.5, 18.9(1.4)<sup>6</sup> ≈ 142, close to the data."], "why": {"0": "The two numbers are switched. The base (growth factor) is the small number near 1.4, and the number in front is the starting amount.", "1": "27 is the value at year 1, not year 0. An exponential model starts at x = 0, one step before, so the front number is smaller than 27 (about 19).", "3": "The two numbers are switched: a base of 27 would mean the houses multiply by 27 every year."}, "right": "a is the year-0 value (before the first row); b is the growth factor near 1.", "desmos": "Add a table with the data, then use <b>Add Regression</b> and pick exponential: a ≈ 18.9, b ≈ 1.4.", "numworks": "<b>Regression</b>: put x in X1 and y in Y1, open the Graph tab, press OK and pick <b>Exponential a·b^x</b>. The Stats tab shows a ≈ 18.9, b ≈ 1.4."},
19:{"how": ["Distribute: 10 − 3<var>m</var> + 15 = 10 − 2<var>m</var>, so 25 − 3<var>m</var> = 10 − 2<var>m</var>.", "<b>Slide</b>: add 3<var>m</var> to both sides: 25 = 10 + <var>m</var>. Subtract 10: <b><var>m</var> = 15</b>."], "miss": "Distribute to both terms: −3(m − 5) is −3m + 15, and 2(5 − m) is 10 − 2m. Then move the m-terms to one side and the numbers to the other.", "right": "Distribute, then slide and slice.", "check": "Put 15 back in: 10 − 3(10) = −20, and 2(5 − 15) = −20. ✓", "numworks": "<b>Equations</b>: type the equation (with =), then <b>Solve the equation</b>: 10 − 3(x − 5) = 2(5 − x) gives x = 15."},
20:{"how": ["Solve for <var>y</var>. <b>Slide</b> the <var>x</var>-term: −<span class=\"frac\"><span>4</span><span>7</span></span><var>y</var> = −<span class=\"frac\"><span>2</span><span>5</span></span><var>x</var> + <span class=\"frac\"><span>3</span><span>2</span></span>.", "<b>Slice</b> by −<span class=\"frac\"><span>4</span><span>7</span></span> (multiply by −<span class=\"frac\"><span>7</span><span>4</span></span>): <var>y</var> = <span class=\"frac\"><span>7</span><span>10</span></span><var>x</var> − <span class=\"frac\"><span>21</span><span>8</span></span>.", "The rate of change is the slope: <b><span class=\"frac\"><span>7</span><span>10</span></span></b>. That is D."], "why": {"0": "−21/8 is the y-intercept after solving for y. The rate of change is the slope, the number with x.", "1": "8/35 comes from multiplying 2/5 and 4/7. Slope is −A/B: (2/5) ÷ (4/7) = 7/10.", "2": "The slope is positive. Dividing −(2/5) by −(4/7) gives a positive 7/10."}, "right": "Rate of change = slope. In Ax + By = C, slope = −A/B.", "desmos": "Type <b>y = (7/10)x − 21/8</b>. Then type the point (5, 7/8) (put x = 5 into the original equation and solve for y): it lands on the line."},
21:{"how": ["Both equations start with <var>x</var>, so <b>subtract</b> them: (<var>x</var> − 3<var>y</var>) − (<var>x</var> − 2<var>y</var>) = 7 − 2 gives −<var>y</var> = 5, so <var>y</var> = −5.", "Put <var>y</var> = −5 into <var>x</var> − 2<var>y</var> = 2: <var>x</var> + 10 = 2, so <b><var>x</var> = −8</b>. That is D."], "why": {"0": "Check it: x = 5 and y = −5 gives 5 − 2(−5) = 15, not 2.", "1": "−5 is the y-value of the solution. The question asks for x.", "2": "Check it: x = 8 and y = −5 gives 8 + 10 = 18, not 2. Watch the sign: x − 2(−5) = x + 10."}, "right": "Subtract to cancel x, find y, then put it back to get x.", "desmos": "Solve both for y: type <b>y = (x − 7)/3</b> and <b>y = (x − 2)/2</b>. Click where they cross: (−8, −5).", "numworks": "<b>Grapher</b>: add both lines in y = form (y = (x − 7)/3 and y = (x − 2)/2), open the Graph tab, press the <b>Toolbox</b> key and choose <b>Intersection</b>: (−8, −5)."},
22:{"how": ["Start value: <b>40</b> employees.", "Increases 7.5% a year: keep 100% + 7.5% = 107.5%, so the base is <b>1.075</b>.", "<var>f</var>(<var>x</var>) = 40(1.075)<sup><var>x</var></sup>. That is B."], "why": {"0": "The company started with 40, not 43. (43 is 40 plus 3, not 7.5% more.)", "2": "The start is 40, and growth of 7.5% means multiply by 1.075. A base of 0.075 would shrink it to almost nothing.", "3": "Growth means the base is 1 + 0.075 = 1.075, not 0.075."}, "right": "a = starting amount; grows r% means b = 1 + r as a decimal.", "desmos": "Type <b>y = 40(1.075)^x</b>. At x = 1 it is 43, which is 7.5% more than 40.", "check": "After 1 year: 40 × 1.075 = 43, and 7.5% of 40 is 3. ✓"},
23:{"how": ["Put each point into 3<var>x</var> − <var>y</var> ≥ 1 and see if it is true.", "(1, 2): 3 − 2 = 1 ≥ 1 ✓.  (4, 0): 12 − 0 = 12 ≥ 1 ✓.  (1, −4): 3 + 4 = 7 ≥ 1 ✓.", "(−3, 2): −9 − 2 = −11, no.  (−3, −3): −9 + 3 = −6, no.", "Select (1, 2), (4, 0) and (1, −4): the point on the line (≥ includes it) and the two to its right."], "miss": "Test each point in the inequality instead of guessing from the picture. (1, 2) is on the line, and ≥ means the line counts. Points to the left of the line make 3x − y too small. You earn 1 point for two correct picks, and an extra pick takes one away.", "right": "Plug the point in. On the line counts when the sign has “or equal.”", "desmos": "The test version won’t graph inequalities. Type the boundary line <b>y = 3x − 1</b> and the points: the solutions are on the line or below/right of it, where 3x − y is bigger."},
24:{"how": ["The rule says each term is <b>−3 plus the term before it</b>: subtract 3 each time.", "Start at <var>a</var><sub>1</sub> = 5: 5, 5 − 3 = 2, 2 − 3 = −1, −1 − 3 = −4.", "5, 2, −1, −4. That is B."], "why": {"0": "The first term is given: a₁ = 5, not −3. The −3 is what you add each time.", "2": "−3 is added each time, not multiplied. And the sequence starts at 5.", "3": "The sequence starts at 5, but each next term is 3 less (−3 + the one before), not 3 more."}, "right": "a₁ is the start; the number added to a(n−1) is the step.", "numworks": "<b>Calculation</b>: type 5, then −3 + Ans three times. You get 2, −1, −4."},
25:{"how": ["<b>Range</b> is the <var>y</var>-values (distance). Read the graph from <b>bottom to top</b>.", "The segment goes from (0, 0) up to (9, 80), so <var>y</var> goes from 0 to 80.", "0 ≤ <var>y</var> ≤ 80. That is C."], "why": {"0": "9 is the biggest x-value (seconds). Range uses y (yards), which goes up to 80.", "1": "This is the domain (x-values, 0 to 9 seconds). Range uses y.", "3": "The numbers are right for range, but range is written with y, not x."}, "right": "Range: bottom to top, written with y."},
26:{"how": ["Factor out −1 first: −(6<var>x</var><sup>2</sup> + 19<var>x</var> − 20).", "6<var>x</var><sup>2</sup> + 19<var>x</var> − 20 = (6<var>x</var> − 5)(<var>x</var> + 4): check the middle: 24<var>x</var> − 5<var>x</var> = 19<var>x</var>. ✓", "So <b>6<var>x</var> − 5</b> is a factor. That is B."], "why": {"0": "(3x − 5) would need a partner (−2x − 4) to give 20 at the end, and the middle would come out wrong. Check: the zero x = 5/3 does not make −6x² − 19x + 20 equal 0.", "2": "x = 5 doesn’t make the expression 0: −150 − 95 + 20 ≠ 0. So x − 5 is not a factor.", "3": "x = 5/2 doesn’t make the expression 0: −37.5 − 47.5 + 20 ≠ 0."}, "right": "A factor’s zero must make the whole expression 0.", "numworks": "<b>Equations</b>: type the equation (with =), then <b>Solve the equation</b>: −6x² − 19x + 20 = 0 gives x = 5/6 and x = −4. 6x − 5 = 0 gives 5/6."},
27:{"how": ["Inside the parentheses, <var>x</var> − 2 moves the graph sideways, the <b>opposite</b> way: <b>right 2</b>.", "Outside, + 5 moves it <b>up 5</b>.", "Right 2 and up 5."], "miss": "Inside with x goes sideways and is backwards: x − 2 means right 2. Outside goes up or down and is normal: + 5 means up 5.", "right": "Inside: sideways, opposite sign. Outside: up/down, same sign.", "desmos": "Type <b>y = x^2</b> and <b>y = (x − 2)^2 + 5</b>. The second one’s lowest point is (2, 5)."},
28:{"how": ["Negative exponent means move it to the other side of the fraction: <var>x</var><sup>−6</sup> on top becomes <var>x</var><sup>6</sup> on the bottom: <span class=\"frac\"><span><var>y</var><sup>4</sup></span><span><var>x</var><sup>3</sup>·<var>x</var><sup>6</sup>·<var>y</var></span></span>.", "<var>x</var>: 3 + 6 = <b>9</b> on the bottom. <var>y</var>: 4 − 1 = <b>3</b> on top.", "<span class=\"frac\"><span><var>y</var><sup>3</sup></span><span><var>x</var><sup>9</sup></span></span>. That is A."], "why": {"1": "The x part is right, but the y on the bottom cancels one y: 4 − 1 = 3.", "2": "Moving x⁻⁶ to the bottom adds to the 3: 3 + 6 = 9, not 3. And y is 4 − 1 = 3.", "3": "The y on the bottom cancels one y (4 − 1 = 3), and x is 3 + 6 = 9, not 2."}, "right": "Negative exponent: switch sides of the fraction. Then subtract exponents.", "numworks": "<b>Calculation</b>: try x = 2, y = 3. Type 2^(−6)×3^(4)÷(2^(3)×3) and 3^(3)÷2^(9) (after each exponent, press the right arrow key). Both show 0.0527."},
29:{"how": ["Line 1: from (−1, −12) to (1, −2): run 2, rise 10, slope 5. Then −2 = 5(1) + b gives b = −7: <b>y = 5x − 7</b>.", "Line 2: from (−1, 16) to (2, 4): run 3, rise −12, slope −4. Then 4 = −4(2) + b gives b = 12: y = −4x + 12, which is <b>4x + y = 12</b>.", "That is B."], "why": {"0": "Line 1 is right, but 4x − y = 4 is y = 4x − 4, and Line 2 goes down (slope −4). Check (−1, 16): 4(−1) − 16 ≠ 4.", "2": "Line 1 has slope 5 (rise 10 over run 2), not 7. Check (1, −2): 7(1) − 9 = −2 works, but (5, 18): 7(5) − 9 = 26, not 18.", "3": "Line 2 is right, but Line 1 has slope 5: (5, 18) gives 5(5) − 7 = 18. ✓ With 7x − 9 you get 26."}, "right": "Slope from two rows, then test a third point in each equation.", "desmos": "Add a table with Line 1’s points, then use <b>Add Regression</b> (linear): m = 5, b = −7. Do the same for Line 2: m = −4, b = 12.", "numworks": "<b>Regression</b>: put x in X1 and y in Y1, open the Graph tab, press OK and pick <b>Linear ax+b</b>. Line 1 gives a = 5, b = −7; Line 2 gives a = −4, b = 12."},
30:{"how": ["Vertex form <var>a</var>(<var>x</var> − <var>h</var>)<sup>2</sup> + <var>k</var>: the vertex is (25, 13).", "<var>a</var> = <span class=\"frac\"><span>2</span><span>5</span></span> is positive, so the parabola opens <b>up</b> and 13 is the <b>lowest</b> <var>y</var>-value.", "Range: all real numbers greater than or equal to 13. That is D."], "why": {"0": "All real numbers is the domain of every quadratic. The range has a lowest value because the parabola opens up.", "1": "25 is the x-value of the vertex. Range is about y-values, and the lowest y is 13.", "2": "10 doesn’t appear in the function. The lowest y-value is k, which is 13."}, "right": "Range of a parabola: y ≥ k when it opens up, y ≤ k when it opens down.", "desmos": "Type <b>y = (2/5)(x − 25)^2 + 13</b> and zoom out. The lowest point is (25, 13)."},
31:{"how": ["Row 1: hotter weather really does make ice cream melt faster, so there is <b>association and causation</b>.", "Row 2: coat sales and temperature move together (association), but buying coats doesn’t make it colder. The cold causes the sales, not the other way around: <b>association only</b>."], "miss": "Association just means the two things change together. Causation means one actually makes the other happen. Cold weather makes people buy coats, but coat sales don’t change the weather.", "right": "Ask: does the first thing actually make the second happen?"},
32:{"how": ["Studio Q: 30 + 80<var>m</var>. Studio R: 105 + 65<var>m</var>.", "Set them equal: 30 + 80<var>m</var> = 105 + 65<var>m</var>. <b>Slide</b>: 15<var>m</var> = 75. <b>Slice</b>: <var>m</var> = 5.", "After <b>5</b> months. That is A."], "why": {"1": "280 is a dollar amount, not a number of months. Set the two costs equal and solve for the months: 15m = 75.", "2": "9 is not where they match. Check: Q = 30 + 80(9) = 750 and R = 105 + 65(9) = 690.", "3": "430 is the total cost at 5 months (30 + 400). The question asks for the number of months."}, "right": "Set the two cost expressions equal and solve.", "check": "5 months: Q = 30 + 400 = 430 and R = 105 + 325 = 430. ✓", "desmos": "Type <b>y = 30 + 80x</b> and <b>y = 105 + 65x</b>. Click where they cross: (5, 430).", "numworks": "<b>Equations</b>: type the equation (with =), then <b>Solve the equation</b>: 30 + 80x = 105 + 65x gives x = 5."},
33:{"how": ["The line crosses the <b><var>x</var>-axis</b> at <b>(6, 0)</b>: the <var>x</var>-intercept is 6. That is B.", "Check the others: it crosses the <var>y</var>-axis at 3, and the slope is down 1 for every 2 right, −<span class=\"frac\"><span>1</span><span>2</span></span>."], "why": {"0": "The line goes down to the right, so the slope is negative: −1/2.", "2": "The zero is the x-intercept, where y = 0: that is x = 6, not 1/2.", "3": "6 is where it crosses the x-axis. The y-intercept is 3."}, "right": "x-intercept on the x-axis; y-intercept on the y-axis.", "desmos": "Type <b>y = −(1/2)x + 3</b>. It crosses the x-axis at (6, 0) and the y-axis at (0, 3)."},
34:{"how": ["16<var>m</var><sup>2</sup> − 81 is a <b>difference of squares</b>: (4<var>m</var>)<sup>2</sup> − 9<sup>2</sup>.", "= (4<var>m</var> − 9)(4<var>m</var> + 9). So <b>4<var>m</var> + 9</b> is a factor. That is D."], "why": {"0": "√16 = 4, so the factors use 4m, not 2m. (2m − 9)(2m + 9) = 4m² − 81.", "1": "16 is a perfect square: 4 × 4. The factors use 4m, not 16m.", "2": "√16 = 4, not 8. (8m − 9)(8m + 9) = 64m² − 81."}, "right": "a² − b² = (a − b)(a + b). Take the square root of each piece.", "numworks": "<b>Equations</b>: type the equation (with =), then <b>Solve the equation</b>: 16x² − 81 = 0 gives x = −9/4 and x = 9/4. 4x + 9 = 0 gives −9/4."},
35:{"how": ["Parallel lines have the <b>same slope</b>. Line <var>k</var> is <var>y</var> = −<var>x</var> + 17, so the slope is <b>−1</b>.", "Use the point (−5, 7): 7 = −1(−5) + <var>b</var>, so 7 = 5 + <var>b</var> and <var>b</var> = <b>2</b>.", "<var>y</var> = −<var>x</var> + 2."], "miss": "Keep the slope −1 (parallel means the same slope, not 17). Then put (−5, 7) in for x and y to find b: −(−5) is +5, so b = 2, not 12. The box already says y =, so type the right side: −x + 2.", "right": "Parallel: same slope. Then plug in the point to find b.", "check": "Put (−5, 7) in: −(−5) + 2 = 7. ✓", "desmos": "Type <b>y = −x + 17</b>, <b>y = −x + 2</b> and the point <b>(−5, 7)</b>. The lines are parallel and the point sits on the second one."},
36:{"how": ["Pick two rows: (15, 3.05) and (20, 3.40).", "<b>Run</b>: 20 − 15 = 5 minutes. <b>Rise</b>: 3.40 − 3.05 = 0.35 dollars.", "Rate = 0.35 ÷ 5 = <b>$0.07 per min</b>. That is C."], "why": {"0": "0.17 is 3.40 ÷ 20, a total cost divided by minutes, which includes the fixed fee. Rate of change is the change in cost over the change in minutes: 0.35 ÷ 5.", "1": "0.35 is the change in cost between 15 and 20 minutes. Divide by the 5 minutes: 0.07.", "3": "0.56 is 2.80 ÷ 5: the change in cost from 15 to 55 minutes, but divided by 5 instead of by the 40 minutes in between. Rise over run: 0.35 ÷ 5 = 0.07."}, "right": "Change in cost ÷ change in minutes.", "desmos": "Add a table with the data, then use <b>Add Regression</b> (linear): m = 0.07.", "numworks": "<b>Regression</b>: put x in X1 and y in Y1, open the Graph tab, press OK and pick <b>Linear ax+b</b>. The Stats tab shows a = 0.07."},
37:{"how": ["Payments: $480 ÷ $96 = 5 payments. So the number of payments goes 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5: <b>domain {0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5}</b> (B).", "Balance after each: 480, 384, 288, 192, 96, 0: <b>range {0, 96, 192, 288, 384, 480}</b> (F).", "Only whole payments make sense, so it is a list of values, not all real numbers."], "why": {"0": "You can’t make 2.5 payments, and there are only 5 of them, so the domain is a short list, not all real numbers.", "2": "Those are dollar amounts (balances), which is the range, not the domain.", "3": "The balance only takes the 6 values 480, 384, 288, 192, 96, 0, not every real number.", "4": "0 to 5 are the numbers of payments (the domain). The range is the balances."}, "right": "Domain = inputs (payments), range = outputs (balances). Real situations often use a list.", "check": "f(5) = 480 − 96(5) = 0, the loan is paid off after 5 payments. ✓"},
38:{"how": ["A product is 0 when one of the factors is 0.", "<var>x</var> + 13 = 0 gives <var>x</var> = <b>−13</b>. <var>x</var> − 6 = 0 gives <var>x</var> = <b>6</b>. That is B."], "why": {"0": "The signs are flipped. x + 13 = 0 means x = −13, and x − 6 = 0 means x = 6.", "2": "The signs are flipped: x + 13 = 0 gives −13 and x − 6 = 0 gives 6.", "3": "x − 6 = 0 gives x = 6, not −6."}, "right": "Set each factor equal to 0; the sign flips.", "numworks": "<b>Equations</b>: type the equation (with =), then <b>Solve the equation</b>: (x + 13)(x − 6) = 0 gives x = −13 and x = 6."},
39:{"how": ["Start value: <b>963</b> grams.", "Decays 27% an hour: keeps 100% − 27% = 73%, so the base is <b>0.73</b>.", "<var>y</var> = 963(0.73)<sup><var>x</var></sup>."], "miss": "Decay means the base is 1 − 0.27 = 0.73 (what is left), not 0.27 and not 1.27. The box already says y =, so type the right side: 963(0.73)^x.", "right": "Decays r% means b = 1 − r as a decimal.", "check": "After 1 hour: 963 × 0.73 = 702.99, which is 27% less than 963. ✓", "desmos": "Type <b>y = 963(0.73)^x</b>. It starts at 963 and drops to about 703 at x = 1."},
40:{"how": ["Solve for <var>y</var>: 3<var>x</var> &lt; 2<var>y</var> + 6 → 3<var>x</var> − 6 &lt; 2<var>y</var> → <var>y</var> &gt; <span class=\"frac\"><span>3</span><span>2</span></span><var>x</var> − 3.", "&lt; with no “or equal” means a <b>dashed</b> line.", "<var>y</var> &gt; means shade <b>above</b> the line. Dashed and shaded above: that is C."], "why": {"0": "The dashed line is right, but y > means shade above the line, not below. Test (0, 0): 0 < 6 is true, so (0, 0) must be in the shading.", "1": "Strict < means a dashed line, and the shading should be above (where (0, 0) is).", "3": "The shading is right, but < with no “or equal” means the line is dashed, not solid."}, "right": "Solve for y. Dashed for < or >. Test (0, 0) for the shading.", "desmos": "The test version won’t graph inequalities. Type the boundary line <b>y = (3/2)x − 3</b>, then test (0, 0) by hand: 0 < 6 is true, so shade the side with (0, 0)."},
41:{"how": ["Rise over run from (−5, −12) to (10, 18).", "<b>Run</b>: 10 − (−5) = 15. <b>Rise</b>: 18 − (−12) = 30.", "Slope = 30 ÷ 15 = <b>2</b>. That is B."], "why": {"0": "This is upside down: rise (30) goes on top, run (15) on the bottom.", "2": "Both points go up to the right, so the slope is positive. Watch the double negatives: 18 − (−12) = 30.", "3": "Upside down and the wrong sign. 30 ÷ 15 = 2."}, "right": "Slope = (y₂ − y₁) ÷ (x₂ − x₁). Subtracting a negative adds.", "desmos": "Add a table with (10, 18) and (−5, −12), then use <b>Add Regression</b> (linear): m = 2.", "numworks": "<b>Regression</b>: put x in X1 and y in Y1, open the Graph tab, press OK and pick <b>Linear ax+b</b>. The Stats tab shows a = 2."},
42:{"how": ["Get 0 on one side: <var>x</var><sup>2</sup> + 3<var>x</var> − 2 = 0. It doesn’t factor, so use the quadratic formula with <var>a</var> = 1, <var>b</var> = 3, <var>c</var> = −2.", "<var>x</var> = <span class=\"frac\"><span>−3 ± √(3<sup>2</sup> − 4(1)(−2))</span><span>2(1)</span></span> = <span class=\"frac\"><span>−3 ± √(9 + 8)</span><span>2</span></span> = <span class=\"frac\"><span>−3 ± √17</span><span>2</span></span>.", "That is C."], "why": {"0": "The formula starts with −b. With b = 3 that is −3, not +3.", "1": "Check: (−1)² − 2 = −1, and −3(−1) = 3. Not equal, so −1 is not a solution. The equation doesn’t factor nicely.", "3": "Check: (−2)² − 2 = 2, and −3(−2) = 6. Not equal."}, "right": "Set it equal to 0 first, then quadratic formula.", "desmos": "Type <b>y = x^2 + 3x − 2</b>. Its x-intercepts are about (−3.56, 0) and (0.56, 0): (−3 − √17)/2 ≈ −3.56 and (−3 + √17)/2 ≈ 0.56.", "numworks": "<b>Equations</b>: type the equation (with =), then <b>Solve the equation</b>: x² − 2 = −3x gives x = (−3 − √17)/2 and x = (−3 + √17)/2."},
43:{"how": ["Square the second part first: (3<var>x</var><sup>−1</sup>)<sup>2</sup> = 9<var>x</var><sup>−2</sup> (power of a power: multiply −1 × 2).", "Multiply: 2 × 9 = <b>18</b>, and <var>x</var><sup>8</sup> · <var>x</var><sup>−2</sup> adds exponents: 8 + (−2) = <b>6</b>.", "18<var>x</var><sup>6</sup>."], "miss": "The square applies to both the 3 and the x⁻¹: 3² = 9 and (x⁻¹)² = x⁻². Then multiply the numbers (2 · 9 = 18) and add the exponents (8 − 2 = 6).", "right": "Power of a power: multiply exponents. Multiplying powers: add exponents.", "numworks": "<b>Calculation</b>: try x = 2. Type (2×2^(8))×(3×2^(−1))^(2) and 18×2^(6) (after each exponent, press the right arrow key). Both show 1152."},
44:{"how": ["Slope from (−1, <span class=\"frac\"><span>27</span><span>7</span></span>) to (3, <span class=\"frac\"><span>11</span><span>7</span></span>): run 4, rise <span class=\"frac\"><span>11</span><span>7</span></span> − <span class=\"frac\"><span>27</span><span>7</span></span> = −<span class=\"frac\"><span>16</span><span>7</span></span>. Slope = −<span class=\"frac\"><span>16</span><span>7</span></span> ÷ 4 = <b>−<span class=\"frac\"><span>4</span><span>7</span></span></b>.", "Find <var>b</var> with (3, <span class=\"frac\"><span>11</span><span>7</span></span>): <span class=\"frac\"><span>11</span><span>7</span></span> = −<span class=\"frac\"><span>4</span><span>7</span></span>(3) + <var>b</var>, so <var>b</var> = <span class=\"frac\"><span>11</span><span>7</span></span> + <span class=\"frac\"><span>12</span><span>7</span></span> = <b><span class=\"frac\"><span>23</span><span>7</span></span></b>.", "<var>y</var> = −<span class=\"frac\"><span>4</span><span>7</span></span><var>x</var> + <span class=\"frac\"><span>23</span><span>7</span></span>. That is A."], "why": {"1": "The slope is right, but b = 11/7 + 12/7 = 23/7, not 31/7.", "2": "The y-values go down as x goes up, so the slope is negative, and it is a small number (−4/7).", "3": "The y-values go down as x goes up, so the slope must be negative."}, "right": "Slope from two rows, then plug a row in to get b.", "desmos": "Add a table with the points (type the fractions as 27/7 and so on), then use <b>Add Regression</b> (linear): m ≈ −0.571 (−4/7) and b ≈ 3.286 (23/7).", "numworks": "<b>Regression</b>: put x in X1 and y in Y1, open the Graph tab, press OK and pick <b>Linear ax+b</b>. The Stats tab shows a ≈ −0.571 (−4/7) and b ≈ 3.286 (23/7)."},
45:{"how": ["Vertex (4, 15) goes in vertex form: <var>f</var>(<var>x</var>) = <var>a</var>(<var>x</var> − 4)<sup>2</sup> + 15.", "Use the point (1, 20): 20 = <var>a</var>(1 − 4)<sup>2</sup> + 15, so 20 = 9<var>a</var> + 15, 5 = 9<var>a</var>, <var>a</var> = <b><span class=\"frac\"><span>5</span><span>9</span></span></b>.", "<var>f</var>(<var>x</var>) = <span class=\"frac\"><span>5</span><span>9</span></span>(<var>x</var> − 4)<sup>2</sup> + 15. That is B."], "why": {"0": "The point (1, 20) is above the vertex (4, 15), so the parabola opens up and a is positive.", "2": "The vertex (4, 15) means + 15 at the end, not − 15. And 35 = 20 + 15 is not how to find a: subtract 15 first.", "3": "The vertex gives + 15, not − 15. From 20 = 9a + 15, a = 5/9."}, "right": "Vertex gives h and k; plug in the other point to find a.", "desmos": "Type <b>y = (5/9)(x − 4)^2 + 15</b> and the point <b>(1, 20)</b>. The point lands on the curve, and the bottom is (4, 15).", "numworks": "<b>Grapher</b>: Add an element, type (5/9)(x − 4)² + 15, open the Graph tab, press the <b>Toolbox</b> key and choose <b>Minimum</b>: (4, 15)."},
46:{"how": ["The line is straight up and down through <var>x</var> = 6: every point has <var>x</var> = 6, so the equation is <b><var>x</var> = 6</b> (VUX: vertical, <var>x</var> =).", "A vertical line has no run, so the slope is <b>undefined</b>. That is D."], "why": {"0": "x = 6 is right, but a vertical line has no run, so its slope is undefined, not 0.", "1": "y = 6 would be a flat (horizontal) line. This line is vertical: x = 6.", "2": "y = 6 would be horizontal with slope 0. This line is vertical: x = 6, undefined slope."}, "right": "HOY: horizontal, 0 slope, y =. VUX: vertical, undefined, x =."},
47:{"how": ["Test each point in <b>both</b> inequalities.", "(−5, 0): <var>y</var> ≥ 2<var>x</var> + 5 → 0 ≥ −5 ✓ and <var>x</var> + <var>y</var> ≤ 1 → −5 ≤ 1 ✓. Both true!", "That is A. (On the graph, (−5, 0) is above the rising line and below the falling line.)"], "why": {"1": "(0, 7): 7 ≥ 5 is true, but 0 + 7 ≤ 1 is false.", "2": "(5, 0): 0 ≥ 15 is false.", "3": "(0, −7): −7 ≥ 5 is false."}, "right": "A solution must make both inequalities true.", "desmos": "The test version won’t graph inequalities. Type the lines <b>y = 2x + 5</b> and <b>y = 1 − x</b> and the point (−5, 0): it is above the first line (y ≥) and below the second (x + y ≤ 1)."},
48:{"how": ["<var>f</var>(−3) = 0 and <var>f</var>(5) = −4 are the points (−3, 0) and (5, −4).", "Slope: run 5 − (−3) = 8, rise −4 − 0 = −4, so slope = −4 ÷ 8 = <b>−0.5</b>.", "Use (−3, 0): 0 = −0.5(−3) + <var>b</var>, so 0 = 1.5 + <var>b</var> and <var>b</var> = <b>−1.5</b>.", "<var>f</var>(<var>x</var>) = −0.5<var>x</var> − 1.5, so the second box is −1.5 (the template shows + )."], "miss": "f(−3) = 0 means the point (−3, 0): the −3 is x and the 0 is y. Slope is −4 ÷ 8 = −0.5 (rise over run). Then plug a point in to get b = −1.5; since the box shows x + __, the second answer is the negative number −1.5.", "right": "f(a) = b is the point (a, b). Slope, then plug in for b.", "desmos": "Add a table with (−3, 0) and (5, −4), then use <b>Add Regression</b> (linear): m = −0.5 and b = −1.5.", "numworks": "<b>Regression</b>: put x in X1 and y in Y1, open the Graph tab, press OK and pick <b>Linear ax+b</b>. The Stats tab shows a = −0.5, b = −1.5."},
49:{"how": ["At <var>x</var> = 0: <var>f</var>(0) = 35(1.4)<sup>0</sup> = 35 · 1 = 35, so the <var>y</var>-intercept is <b>(0, 35)</b>. That is A.", "Base 1.4 is more than 1, so the graph is <b>increasing</b>, and an exponential graph never touches the <var>x</var>-axis (asymptote <var>y</var> = 0)."], "why": {"1": "An exponential function like this never reaches y = 0, so it has no x-intercept. 1.4 is the growth factor.", "2": "The base 1.4 is bigger than 1, so the graph goes up (increasing), not down.", "3": "The asymptote of this function is y = 0, the x-axis. 0.4 is just the part of 1.4 above 1 (the 40% growth)."}, "right": "y-intercept is a; b > 1 goes up; asymptote y = 0.", "desmos": "Type <b>y = 35(1.4)^x</b>. It crosses the y-axis at 35, goes up, and flattens along y = 0 on the left."},
50:{"how": ["Find two numbers that multiply to −6 and add to −5: <b>−6 and 1</b>.", "(<var>k</var> − 6)(<var>k</var> + 1). Check the middle: <var>k</var> − 6<var>k</var> = −5<var>k</var>. ✓ That is C."], "why": {"0": "(k − 1)(k + 6) = k² + 5k − 6. The middle sign is wrong.", "1": "(k − 3)(k + 2) = k² − k − 6. The middle should be −5k.", "3": "(k − 2)(k + 3) = k² + k − 6. The middle should be −5k."}, "right": "Multiply to c, add to b. Check the middle term.", "numworks": "<b>Equations</b>: type the equation (with =), then <b>Solve the equation</b>: x² − 5x − 6 = 0 gives x = 6 and x = −1, which match (k − 6) and (k + 1)."}
};
